 that  one of the CEOs taught me that I have  found to be very useful as never bring  cold issues to the board table so any  hard especially hard decisions hard  issues that we want to tackle I always  make it a point to kind of get off line  input from individual board members and  get that perspective on it so that when  we go to the table you know we can  actually have a productive discussion  and we can start with respective few  points versus arriving at them and I  think someone's said giving up the you  should give up controlling the board I  have one comment for that which is  either you manage your board or they  manage you so that's the CEO perspective  of it which is that you need to set the  agenda you need to be very clear to  communicate what the company's viewpoint  on the team's view point out to the  board with that I'll give my thanks pod  knee last up is Kevin F rosy from Excel  who has the enviable challenge of not  being redundant with the first four  panelists Kevin you haven't well before  I get started I want to know my audience  a little bit here before I kind of  launch into some slopes locally how many  of you guys are interested in this  because you're thinking of being  prospective entrepreneurs okay how many  of you guys are thinking of being  prospective board members well okay  sorry board members but not on not  entrepreneurs on other people's boards  okay all right so you know I'll skew it  more towards the entrepreneur but then  I'll try to address a little bit about  you know what you should think of it as  a board member I've been both and so but  I've been on the entrepreneur side and  now I have a pleasure of being on the  other side the dark side so you know and  I'll try to be brief here I think the  main thing for the entrepreneur is you  need to lead that the comment manager  border they will manage you what that  really means is is think about the way  your investors in particular look at  your business and the way we look at  your business is an entrepreneur and it  should be this  any way you look at your business by the  way as a start-up which is what are the  key risks to my business what are the  and there shouldn't be more than three  or you shouldn't cut fund it but there  should there probably three big risks  that you want to take off the table and  I'll try to make this concrete as an  example so the second company I started  as a conical iron planet you know eBay  for construction equipment type of thing  we can also a smile at that idea it's  actually doing pretty well now but when  I got this thing started I said well you  know what are my three top priorities  because we've started from a blank sheet  of paper at a time it was one I needed  you know to recruit the team it's pretty  simple recruit the team launch the  product and the third I had to take out  supply risk which I saw is toward the  big risk of my business I had to get a  whole bunch of supply on the business to  launch and that enabled signs you know  there was a major strategic partnership  I need to sign those are the three  things and how is it and I said you know  what I'm just not going to worry about  everything else you know real estate and  branding and all this others but just  can't focus on it and I got sort of  buying for my board in individual means  and these are the three things I need to  worry about and that's all we're going  to talk about in the board meetings it's  not going to be in the firt until I get  the next stage I accomplish those three  objectives and then you figure out what  are the next three key risks to get you  to the next level so the way we as  venture capitals think about it is what  are the you know what are the areas of  risk we need to take out so that when we  raise another round of financing it's  going to be an up around you know very  simply put so and run your board  meetings in that way as opposed to  making them some you know parade of your  operational executives giving a progress  update because you know that way you  keep your board members focused you say  you remember here the three priorities  let's keep the conversations to those  things I can't emphasize enough how  important that is because your board  members will distract you  and work on the issues that are  interesting to them if you don't lead  them what to look for and recruiting  your board as an entrepreneur to be  honest a lot of it is people who are  willing to will do the  work and work hard in an early stage  people are willing to do the work and  all these other things are important but  if you don't have someone's going to do  the work no the other stuff matters and  it's a lot of work some other areas of  that and make sure their interests are  aligned with yours don't have strategic  investors on your board if you can avoid  it you just don't need the conflict and  they can't it's very difficult from the  where their board member hat and take  off their strategic investor hat so I  would encourage you to not have anybody  on your board who isn't strategically  aligned and the problem is you think  they can help there are relevant partner  in your industry whatever they shouldn't  be on your board would be my advice you  can't always avoid it  but I would look to avoid it and then  I'd say you know after that it's all the  other obvious things you know they have  a relevant network and you know I agree  with the comment about the senior  luminary that Leonard made for the most  part except there are some exceptions to  the rule we have a senior luminary who's  willing to do the work and I've been on  support so they had a tremendous amount  of value but I've also been on others  where they don't do squat now as a board  member what would be the advice I would  have I would tend to agree with with  with Leonard the most a lot of his  comments the board the board's job is  not to run your company and so back to  being an entrepreneur when you're  